entry.summary:
Spent a lot of time debugging this.
I was using react color library from NPM in a project and my project was in ES module scope.
import reactColor from "react-color";

TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'displayName')

import * as reactColor from "react-color";

const { SketchPicker } = reactColor;

A practical guide to the entries() iterator on arrays, NodeLists, and more — with comparisons, do’s & don’ts, and an illustration prompt.
entries() is a method (list.entries()), not a property (list.entries).
It returns an iterator that yields [index, value] pairs.
Use for…of or the iterator protocol; never use for…in to walk it.
Exists on Array, NodeList, Map, Set, and more.
If you don’t need the index, iterate directly with for…of or values() instead.
entries() return?





const links = document.querySelectorAll('.article a');
for (const [i, el] of links.entries()) {
  console.log(i, el.href);
}
//→ 0 "https://…"   1 "https://…"

entries() gives back an iterator object whose .next() method delivers an object shaped like:
{ value: [index, element], done: false }

When done becomes true, …
